2026-08-23 · THIRD PASS: four bugs from one screenshot, and line kinds
===========================================================================

Reported with a screenshot of the real thing, which found in one look what a
suite with no terminal could not find at all.


1. THE `Working...^M` FLOOD
---------------------------
`console.status` is a rich Live. It redraws by emitting `\r\x1b[2K` and the
line again, about twelve times a second. `LogPane` split on `\n` only, so
EVERY SPINNER FRAME BECAME A ROW - thousands of them, pushing the session off
the screen.

The pane now honours `\r` the way a terminal does: it replaces the line being
built. Verified against the exact bytes rich emits - 200 frames collapse to one
row. npm, pip and pytest progress output all redraw the same way and are fixed
by the same change.


2. THE SCRAMBLED SIDEBAR - the serious one
------------------------------------------
Sidebar labels came out as `Mcontext`, `MPROGRESS`, `Midle`, and the frame was
mangled around them.

`ANSI()` consumes the SGR codes it understands and PASSES THE REST THROUGH AS
TEXT. prompt_toolkit writes fragment text verbatim. So a `\x1b[1A` sitting in
the pane was physically moving the real cursor, and everything drawn after it
landed in the wrong place - sidebar included.

    'p\x1b[1Aq'   -> 'pq'      (consumed, harmless)
    'z\x1b[?25lw' -> 'z25lw'   <- literal garbage: ANSI does not know `?`
    'a\rb'        -> 'a\rb'    <- raw CR, reaches the terminal

Two guards: private-mode CSI (`ESC [ ? ... `) is stripped BEFORE `ANSI()` sees
it, and every remaining C0 control character is dropped after. Nothing that can
steer a cursor is ever stored.


3. A ONE-LINE INPUT BOX
-----------------------
Fine for "fix the tests", useless for what actually starts a real turn - a PRD,
a traceback, a spec with eight bullets. The buffer is multiline now: Enter
submits, Alt+Enter opens a line (the same contract as the idle prompt, so the
two boxes behave alike), and the box grows to `INPUT_MAX_ROWS` = 8 before it
scrolls internally.


4. SERVICES IN THE SIDEBAR
--------------------------
    SERVICES
    model     qwen3.5:9b
    telegram  configured
    web       off

Real readings: `poller_status()` for the bot (running / configured / not set
up), the web portal manager for its URL, the tier for the model. SAMPLED, not
polled - the sidebar repaints five times a second and these come from a SQLite
lease table; a query per 200ms for something that changes when you type a
command would be absurd. `SERVICES_TTL_SECONDS = 5`. Anything unreachable reads
a grey `unknown`: an exception raised inside a renderer takes the frame down.

Deliberately NOT in `TurnTelemetry` - everything there is fed by the turn
stream and belongs to one turn, while these outlive turns and come from state
other PROCESSES own. Mixing them would make the telemetry reach into a
database, which is exactly what a renderer must not do.


5. TELLING A PROMPT, A LOG AND AN ANSWER APART
----------------------------------------------
Three different kinds of thing arriving as one undifferentiated wall.

    › the server crashes when REDIS_URL is missing        <- prompt, cli
      model responded in 11s                              <- log
      Reading config.py - 14ms                            <- log
    ◆ Fixed - config.py falls back to redis://localhost   <- the answer
    ✈ also log a warning when it falls back               <- prompt, telegram
    ◈ now run the tests                                   <- prompt, web

A prompt is coloured by WHERE IT CAME FROM. The same session takes work from
this terminal, the web portal and Telegram, and "who asked for this?" was
otherwise unanswerable from the log. `TurnEvent.source` already carried it.

  cli       green    ›
  web       blue     ◈
  telegram  magenta  ✈
  unknown   amber    ?
  answer    white    ◆
  log       whatever rich sent - the neutral background the others stand out
            against, which only works because the others are the minority.

Every surface differs in BOTH channels. Colour alone is not a distinction:
palettes vary, some are unreadable, and some people cannot tell the green from
the amber - so the mark sits in the same column on every row and the shape of
the conversation survives a screenshot with the colour stripped out.

`decorate_as` is a context manager rather than an argument on `write`, because
the writes it has to cover are `console.print` calls made by code that has
never heard of a pane. It closes any half-written line on the way in, or a
chunk still open would inherit a mark belonging to the next thing.

A turn begun on another surface is echoed here through
`TuiApp.absorb_for_display`, registered on the same stream as the telemetry.
The CLI's own prompt is skipped there because `FrameHost.submit` echoed it at
the moment it was typed.


