All three were already measurable and none were shown anywhere.


TOKENS PER SECOND, FROM OLLAMA'S OWN TIMINGS
--------------------------------------------
Not a stopwatch around the call. `eval_duration` is GENERATION ONLY - no
queueing, no HTTP round trip, no prompt evaluation - so `eval_count /
eval_duration` is the honest rate. `_record_usage` already read `eval_count`
and threw the durations away.

    eval_duration is in NANOSECONDS.

240 tokens in 8_000_000_000 ns is 30 tok/s. Read as seconds it would be 30
nanoseconds and the rate would be astronomical, which is the kind of unit bug
that ships looking plausible - so it has its own test with the arithmetic
written out.

Coloured, because the number exists to catch one specific fall:

    < 8 tok/s    red      the model has almost certainly spilled to the CPU
    < 20 tok/s   amber
    >= 20        green

A 7-9B at q4 on this hardware runs in the tens on the card and in low single
digits off it. That gap was previously invisible until a turn simply took
twenty minutes.

An untimed response does NOT blank the last reading - same rule as
`last_prompt_tokens`: being told nothing is not the same as being told zero,
and the display reads zero as "unknown". Across SESSIONS it does not carry,
because that is the leak `_COUNTERS_BY_SESSION` exists to prevent.


VRAM, FROM /api/ps
------------------
The most consequential number on this machine and the one nobody could see.
Ollama reserves the KV cache for the WHOLE context window up front, so a window
that does not fit spills to the CPU and the same turn takes six times as long.

    {"size_vram": 6207559433, "context_length": 32768}   ->  6.2G · 32k

    loaded          green
    size_vram = 0   RED - it is resident but running on the CPU
    no models       grey "nothing loaded"
    unreachable     grey "unknown"

"Loaded, 6.2G" and "not loaded" are different worlds and the log said neither.


MEMORY
------
`psutil.Process().memory_info().rss` - SHAMSU's own footprint, not Ollama's.
`psutil>=5.9` is already a declared dependency.


WHY THESE ARE IN `Services` AND NOT IN `TurnTelemetry`
------------------------------------------------------
Everything in `TurnTelemetry` is fed by the turn stream and belongs to ONE
TURN. VRAM and RSS outlive turns and come from state other processes own, so
they are sampled on the 5-second TTL with the rest of the services. Speed is
the exception and lives with the turn, because it is a property of the calls
this turn made - and it arrives the way every other turn number does, on the
status event, so the sidebar never reaches back into the loop.


